# Line endings, settled once for the whole repo.
#
# Reported by Ancung: `pnpm test` could not go green on Windows at all. Git for
# Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, so checkout rewrote
# sdk/schema/race-record-v1.0.json to CRLF while src/schema.ts kept generating
# LF — and that test compares the two BYTE FOR BYTE on purpose, because the
# published JSON Schema has to be exactly what the validator generates. `git
# status` stayed clean, so it looked like a code bug and was not one. CI is
# Ubuntu, so it never showed there.
#
# The fix is repo-wide rather than one line for that one file, because this repo
# has several places where bytes are the contract:
#
#   sdk/schema/*.json      compared byte-for-byte against generated output, and
#                          shipped in the npm tarball
#   sdk/vendor/*.ts        must stay byte-identical to sc/bindings/*/src/index.ts
#   docs/specs/**          frozen artefacts; the vectors are the spec
#   *.sh                   a shell script with CRLF fails on Linux with
#                          "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M", which is a
#                          genuinely baffling error the first time
#   Dockerfile, *.yml      built and run on Linux
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` means: Git decides what is text, normalises it to LF in the
# repository, and checks it out as LF everywhere — including Windows. Nothing
# here is Windows-specific, so nothing wants CRLF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Declared rather than left to auto-detection. Auto-detection is good and these
# are unambiguous, but a corrupted PNG from a line-ending conversion is an
# expensive thing to debug, and the cost of being explicit is four lines.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.jpg binary
*.woff2 binary

# Generator output, and the lockfiles. Marked so `git diff` stops offering to
# show a thousand-line change nobody reads.
pnpm-lock.yaml       -diff linguist-generated
sc/bindings/*/package-lock.json -diff linguist-generated
sdk/vendor/*.ts      linguist-generated
